(Other scripts are spelt in Latin letters here, as the Amiga shows them;
docs/guide.md and docs/reference.md have the original characters.)

utf8.library programmer's guide
===============================

utf8.library lets an Amiga program work with UTF-8, the text of the web,
of mail, of every modern computer, while the Amiga itself speaks Latin-1.
It counts, measures, cuts, searches, compares and converts UTF-8 text, and
turns it into and out of Latin-1 and thirteen other 8-bit sets. It works
from C (and any language that can call a library) and from ARexx.

This guide explains how to use it. Every call is described in full in the
reference. Complete working programs: tests/first.c (this guide's first
program), tests/utf8test.c (every call, C) and tests/utf8test.rexx
(ARexx).

  - What you need
  - The first program
  - How text is passed
  - Bytes, code points, characters, columns
  - Text in a terminal
  - An editor's cursor
  - Case, accents, searching and sorting
  - Character sets
  - Accents together or apart
  - ARexx
  - Speed
  - Versions
  - Rules and traps



WHAT YOU NEED
-------------

  - AmigaOS 2.04 or newer, any 680x0. The library calls nothing from the
    OS but exec, so it should also run on 1.3, but it has not been tested
    there.
  - LIBS:utf8.library: the build for the processor (build/000, 020 or 060,
    see the README).
  - The headers in include/: libraries/utf8.h (constants), proto/utf8.h,
    inline/utf8.h (the calls, for gcc) and clib/utf8_protos.h (the
    prototypes). utf8.sfd describes the calls and their registers, for
    making headers for other languages (sfdc or fd2pragma).
  - Your program's stack: the library's deepest call uses under 0.5 KB.



THE FIRST PROGRAM
-----------------

Three lines of UTF-8, shown on an Amiga console, measured, and searched:

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <proto/exec.h>
    #include <proto/utf8.h>

    struct Library *Utf8Base;

    int main(void)
    {
        static const char *lines[] = {
            "G\xc3\xb6teborg \xe2\x80\x93 \xe2\x80\x9cLittle London\xe2\x80\x9d, 5 \xe2\x82\xac",
            "\xc5\x81\xc3\xb3" "d\xc5\xba, Krak\xc3\xb3w, Gda\xc5\x84sk",
            "\xe6\x9d\xb1\xe4\xba\xac means Tokyo \xf0\x9f\x97\xbc",
        };
        char shown[80];
        int i;

        if (!(Utf8Base = OpenLibrary(UTF8NAME, 0))) {
            printf("needs utf8.library\n");
            return 20;
        }
        for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            Utf8ToCharset(lines[i], -1, shown, sizeof(shown), U8CS_LATIN1, 0);
            printf("%-34s %2ld characters, %2ld columns\n", shown,
                   (long)Utf8Length(lines[i], -1), (long)Utf8Width(lines[i], -1));
        }
        if (Utf8Find(lines[0], -1, "goteborg", -1, U8F_NOCASE | U8F_NOACCENT, NULL) >= 0)
            printf("\"goteborg\" found without case or accents\n");
        CloseLibrary(Utf8Base);
        return 0;
    }

Built with m68k-amigaos-gcc -noixemul -Wno-pointer-sign -Iinclude first.c
-o first, it prints (on an A1200 in FS-UAE, 2026-10-17):

    Göteborg - "Little London", 5 EUR  31 characters, 31 columns
    Lódz, Kraków, Gdansk               20 characters, 20 columns
    ?? means Tokyo ?                   16 characters, 19 columns
    "goteborg" found without case or accents

The Amiga's Latin-1 has ö and ó but not -, " ", EUR, L, z or n:
Utf8ToCharset() gives the nearest it has (- " EUR L z n). Tokyo's two
characters and the tower emoji have nothing near, so they are ?, but they
still take two columns each, which is why the third line is 16 characters
and 19 columns.



HOW TEXT IS PASSED
------------------

Every call takes text the same way: a pointer and a length in bytes, where
a length below 0 means "up to the 0 byte". So a whole C string is (s, -1),
and a part of a buffer is (buf + 10, 25) with no 0 needed.

A byte that is not UTF-8 is a Latin-1 letter. UTF-8 has strict rules; a
byte that breaks them (a stray 0xE5 from an Amiga file, a sequence cut
short) is not an error but the Latin-1 character with that number, as it
would be on the Amiga. So "G\xf6teborg" (Latin-1) and "G\xc3\xb6teborg"
(UTF-8) are both Göteborg to every call: they have the same length,
compare equal, and convert to the same Latin-1. A program can hand the
library text without knowing which it is.

The calls that write text (Utf8Case, Utf8Normalize, Utf8ToCharset,
Utf8FromCharset) all work like C's snprintf: they write at most size - 1
bytes and a 0, and return the length the whole result needs. Their output
is never cut in the middle of a character. Measure first if the result's
size is not known:

    LONG n = Utf8FromCharset(amigatext, -1, NULL, 0, U8CS_LATIN1);     /* how big */
    STRPTR u8 = AllocVec(n + 1, MEMF_ANY);
    if (u8) Utf8FromCharset(amigatext, -1, u8, n + 1, U8CS_LATIN1);    /* and write */

UTF-8 output from the library is always good UTF-8, whatever came in.



BYTES, CODE POINTS, CHARACTERS, COLUMNS
---------------------------------------

Four different counts, and mixing them up is the classic UTF-8 bug:

  Text                     Bytes  Code points  Characters  Columns
  -----------------------  -----  -----------  ----------  -------
  abc                      3      3            3           3
  Göteborg                 9      8            8           8
  é written as e + accent  3      2            1           1
  ??                       6      2            2           4
  SE (a flag)              8      2            1           2
  ??? (a family)         18     5            1           2

  - Bytes: strlen(). For buffers and file sizes.
  - Code points: what Utf8Decode() steps through. Unicode's numbers.
  - Characters: what a reader counts, and what the cursor should move
    over. Utf8Length(), Utf8Next(), Utf8Prev(). A letter with its accents,
    a flag, an emoji family is one.
  - Columns: how much room it takes on a terminal or in any fixed-width
    font. Utf8Width(), Utf8Fit(), Utf8CharWidth(). Chinese, Japanese and
    Korean characters and most emoji take two.



TEXT IN A TERMINAL
------------------

A program drawing in a console or terminal window (CTerm, or anything
built on tui.library) needs columns, not bytes:

    LONG w = Utf8Width(title, -1);
    LONG col = (80 - w) / 2;                                   /* centred */

    LONG fit = Utf8Fit(line, -1, 80, &w);                      /* what fits in 80 columns */
    Write(out, line, fit);                                     /* never half a character */

    LONG at = Utf8Fit(line, -1, mousecol, NULL);               /* the byte a click landed on */

Utf8Width() counts controls (tab, escape, line ends) as 0 columns: where a
tab stops is the program's own business.



AN EDITOR'S CURSOR
------------------

Utf8Next() and Utf8Prev() move by whole characters, so the cursor never
lands inside one and backspace removes an é whether it is one code point
or two:

    pos = Utf8Next(text, len, pos);                    /* cursor right */
    pos = Utf8Prev(text, len, pos);                    /* cursor left */

    start = Utf8Prev(text, len, pos);                  /* backspace: remove start..pos */
    memmove(text + start, text + pos, len - pos);
    len -= pos - start;
    pos = start;

For moving by words, Utf8Class() tells letters from spaces and punctuation
in every script; a word is a run of characters with UC_WORD:

    ULONG c;
    while (pos < len) {                                /* to the end of this word */
        Utf8Decode(text + pos, len - pos, &c);
        if (!(Utf8Class(c) & UC_WORD)) break;
        pos = Utf8Next(text, len, pos);
    }



CASE, ACCENTS, SEARCHING AND SORTING
------------------------------------

  - One character: Utf8Upper(), Utf8Lower(), Utf8NoAccent() (é to e, L to
    L, Y to I).
  - A whole text: Utf8Case() with U8F_UPPER or U8F_LOWER.
  - Searching: Utf8Find() gives where a text is found and how many bytes
    the match is, with U8F_NOCASE and U8F_NOACCENT if wanted. With both,
    "malmo" finds "Malmö". Use the length it gives, not the search text's,
    since "o" matching "ö" is one byte against two.
  - Comparing and sorting: Utf8Compare(), with the same flags. The order
    is Unicode's numbers, good for file names; it is not a dictionary's
    alphabet for any one language.

Both Utf8Find() and Utf8Compare() take an accent written as one character
and as a letter plus a separate mark as the same, so text from a Mac
matches text from an Amiga.



CHARACTER SETS
--------------

The Amiga's own text is Latin-1 (ISO-8859-1). Other Amigas and other
computers have used other 8-bit sets, and the library knows fourteen:

  ISO-8859-1
    Number: U8CS_LATIN1
    Used by: the Amiga, western Europe
  ISO-8859-2
    Number: U8CS_LATIN2
    Used by: Central Europe: Polish, Czech, Hungarian Amigas
  ISO-8859-15
    Number: U8CS_LATIN9
    Used by: Latin-1 with EUR
  ISO-8859-5
    Number: U8CS_CYRILLIC
    Used by: Cyrillic
  ISO-8859-7
    Number: U8CS_GREEK
    Used by: Greek
  ISO-8859-9
    Number: U8CS_LATIN5
    Used by: Turkish
  windows-1250, 1251, 1252
    Number: U8CS_CP1250, CP1251, CP1252
    Used by: Windows text files
  IBM437
    Number: U8CS_CP437
    Used by: the PC's own: BBSes, ANSI art, DOS
  IBM850, IBM866
    Number: U8CS_CP850, CP866
    Used by: DOS western and Cyrillic
  KOI8-R
    Number: U8CS_KOI8R
    Used by: Russian
  macintosh
    Number: U8CS_MACROMAN
    Used by: old Macintosh files
  US-ASCII
    Number: U8CS_ASCII
    Used by: 7-bit: everything else as its nearest
  UTF-8
    Number: U8CS_UTF8
    Used by: a checked copy, so one code path serves every set

  - UTF-8 in, Amiga out: Utf8ToCharset(text, len, buf, size, U8CS_LATIN1,
    0). What Latin-1 lacks becomes the nearest it has. With U8F_STRICT it
    becomes ? instead, for when a guess would be wrong (a file name to
    write back).
  - Other scripts spelt out (0.1b2): where the set lacks them, Cyrillic,
    Greek, Japanese kana and Korean come out in Latin letters, as signs
    and maps write them for those who cannot read the script: "Privet,
    mir" gives "Privet, mir", "Omega" "Omega", "konnichiha" "konnichiha",
    "koohii" "koohii", "annyeonghaseyo" "annyeonghaseyo". Russian as people usually
    write it (zh zh, shch shch, y y), Greek letter by letter, kana in Hepburn
    (small kana join the one before: kya kya,  doubles the next consonant,
    - repeats the vowel), Korean in the Revised Romanization, letter by
    letter. Chinese characters and the kanji in Japanese have no spelling
    without a dictionary and stay ?, as do emoji (a flag gives its two
    letters, SE SE). A set that has the letters keeps them: Cyrillic into
    U8CS_CYRILLIC stays Cyrillic. A spelt letter can be longer than its
    UTF-8 (shch is two bytes, "shch" four; a Korean syllable is three, its
    spelling up to seven), so the result can be longer than the text:
    measure first, or give room.
  - Amiga in, UTF-8 out: Utf8FromCharset(text, len, buf, size,
    U8CS_LATIN1).
  - A set by its name: a mail's charset=iso-8859-2, an HTTP header, an FTP
    server's UTF8: Utf8Charset("iso-8859-2") gives U8CS_LATIN2, -1 for a
    name it does not know. Utf8CharsetName() gives the names back, for a
    menu of sets.
  - Not sure what a file is: Utf8Check() says U8_ASCII, U8_UTF8, or
    U8_LATIN1 (has bytes that are not UTF-8, so it is 8-bit text).



ACCENTS TOGETHER OR APART
-------------------------

Unicode can write å two ways: as one character (U+00E5), the way Amigas,
Windows and the web do, or as an a followed by a combining ring (U+0061
U+030A), the way Macs may store file names. They look the same but are
different bytes.

The library treats the two as the same everywhere it compares
(Utf8Compare, Utf8Find) and puts the accent on its letter when converting
(Utf8ToCharset), so a Mac's file name still becomes Latin-1's å.
Utf8Normalize() writes text either way: U8F_COMPOSE puts every accent on
its letter, U8F_DECOMPOSE takes them all off. This covers Latin, Greek,
Cyrillic, Japanese kana and Korean Hangul, and gives the same as Unicode's
NFC and NFD there.



AREXX
-----

The library is also an ARexx function library:

    CALL ADDLIB('utf8.library', 0, -30, 0)
    SAY UTF8TO(UTF8UPPER(text))                    /* show UTF-8 text on the Amiga */

ARexx's own LENGTH, SUBSTR, LEFT, POS and UPPER count bytes and know only
Latin-1. These count whole characters (from 1, as ARexx does) and know all
of Unicode:

  UTF8LEN(text)
    how many characters
  UTF8WIDTH(text)
    how many columns
  UTF8LEFT(text, columns)
    the start that fits in the columns
  UTF8SUBSTR(text, start, [count])
    characters from start (1 = the first), count of them or all the rest
  UTF8UPPER(text), UTF8LOWER(text)
    in upper or lower case
  UTF8TO(text, [set])
    UTF-8 into a set (Latin-1 if none given), the nearest for what it
      lacks
  UTF8FROM(text, [set])
    a set's text (Latin-1 if none) into UTF-8
  UTF8NORMALIZE(text, ['NFD'])
    accents on their letters, or with NFD apart
  UTF8CHECK(text)
    ASCII, UTF8 or LATIN1
  UTF8FIND(text, find, ['NOCASE NOACCENT'])
    the character where find starts, 0 if nowhere
  UTF8COMPARE(a, b, ['NOCASE NOACCENT'])
    -1, 0 or 1
  UTF8CHAR(code, [code...])
    the characters as UTF-8; codes as 229, U+00E5, 0xE5 or $E5
  UTF8CODE(text, [n])
    the n-th code point (from 1) as a number

A set is named as for Utf8Charset(): 'latin2', 'CP437', 'windows-1252'. A
wrong argument gives ARexx error 18, a missing one error 17.



SPEED
-----

Measured with tests/utf8test (4 KB of text, each call for 2 seconds),
FS-UAE A1200, 68EC020 at 14 MHz with fast RAM, OS 3.2.1, the 020 build
(0.1b2):

  Call                     Swedish   English   Japanese + emoji
  -----------------------  --------  --------  ----------------
  Utf8Width                131 KB/s  118 KB/s  28 KB/s
  Utf8Length               159 KB/s  150 KB/s  37 KB/s
  Utf8ToCharset (Latin-1)  196 KB/s  318 KB/s  87 KB/s
  Utf8Find (NOCASE)        125 KB/s  200 KB/s  12 KB/s

A plain C loop that only adds up the bytes ran at 444 KB/s on the same
machine. A 68040 is about four times faster, and an A1200 with Emu68 on a
PiStorm about a hundred times faster (12-57 MB/s for Latin text, 0.1b1).

On a 68000 (FS-UAE A600, 7 MHz, chip RAM only, Kickstart 3.1, the 000
build), Utf8ToCharset into Latin-1, 25-30 KB of each:

  Text                        0.1b1    0.1b2
  --------------------------  -------  -------
  English (ASCII)             51 KB/s  89 KB/s
  Swedish and German letters  12 KB/s  34 KB/s
  Russian                     1 KB/s   16 KB/s
  Japanese kana               1 KB/s   12 KB/s
  Chinese characters          1 KB/s   27 KB/s
  Korean                      2 KB/s   14 KB/s

Into Latin-1 and ASCII, Utf8ToCharset takes ASCII, Latin-1's letters,
Cyrillic, Greek, kana, Hangul, Chinese characters, the common punctuation,
box lines and emoji in one tight loop when no accent follows to join them;
everything else, and every other set, goes the full way. Both give the
same for every code point (make lanecheck).



VERSIONS
--------

While the library is 0.x, lib_Version is 0 and the revision counts up as
calls are added (0.1, 0.2...). Open it with version 0 and check
lib_Revision before using a call newer than 0.1: the reference gives each
call's revision. From 1.0 on the version goes up instead, as usual.



RULES AND TRAPS
---------------

  - Utf8Base is the library base (struct Library *Utf8Base;, as
    proto/utf8.h expects).
  - Lengths are bytes, everywhere, also where the call counts characters
    or columns.
  - Positions are bytes. Utf8Next(), Utf8Prev() and Utf8Find() give byte
    positions; ARexx's functions give character positions from 1.
  - A cut buffer: a file read in pieces may end in the middle of a
    character. Hold back the bytes of a character cut short and put them
    in front of the next piece:

c /* k: how many bytes at the end of buf (len bytes) start a character cut
short */ LONG k = 0, i; for (i = 1; i <= 3 && i <= len; i++) { UBYTE b =
buf[len - i]; if ((b & 0xC0) == 0x80) continue; /* inside a character */
if (b >= 0xC2 && b <= 0xF4 && i < (b < 0xE0 ? 2 : b < 0xF0 ? 3 : 4)) k =
i; break; }

  - char and STRPTR: gcc warns about signedness when a char * is passed,
    as with every Amiga call that takes a STRPTR. -Wno-pointer-sign
    quietens it.
  - One letter, one letter: case and accent calls never change the number
    of characters, so ß has no upper case here (Unicode's is SS) and fi
    stays one ligature.
  - The tables are Unicode 16.0 (2024): widths, case and classes for every
    code point, checked against Python's Unicode database for all
    1,114,112 of them.

